---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Methodology — UK Net Zero Tracker"
  description="How the UK Net Zero Tracker scores progress, sources its data, and handles uncertainty."
>
  <div class="meth-page">

    <div class="page-intro">
      <p class="page-label">Methodology</p>
      <h1 class="page-heading">How this works</h1>
      <p class="page-sub">
        Every figure on this dashboard has a source, a refresh cadence, and a
        written rationale for how it's scored — or a written rationale for why
        it isn't scored. This page explains all of that. It's written to be
        readable without a background in energy policy, but it doesn't
        oversimplify for readers who do have one.
      </p>
    </div>

    <nav class="meth-nav" aria-label="Methodology sections">
      <a href="#how-we-score">How we score</a>
      <a href="#card-types">Card types</a>
      <a href="#constraint-costs">Grid constraint costs</a>
      <a href="#versioning">Versioning</a>
    </nav>

    <section class="meth-section" id="how-we-score">
      <h2 class="section-heading">How we score infrastructure metrics</h2>

      <p>
        For each infrastructure metric, the dashboard asks one question: <em>at the current build rate, will we hit the 2030 target?</em>
      </p>

      <p>
        We don't score absolute capacity, and we don't score year-on-year
        change. We score the projected outcome at the deadline against the
        committed target for that deadline.
      </p>

      <h3 class="sub-heading">The method: gap-closure projection</h3>

      <p>For each metric:</p>

      <ol class="meth-list">
        <li>Take the trailing 12 months of additions to operational capacity — not consented, not under construction, only what's actually running.</li>
        <li>Take the gap between current operational capacity and the 2030 target.</li>
        <li>Take the years remaining until end of 2030.</li>
        <li>Project: <code>current capacity + (trailing 12-month rate × years remaining)</code>.</li>
        <li>Score: <code>projected 2030 capacity ÷ 2030 target = delivery ratio</code>.</li>
      </ol>

      <p>The delivery ratio determines the traffic light.</p>

      <div class="threshold-table-wrap">
        <table class="threshold-table">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Signal</th>
              <th>Delivery ratio</th>
              <th>What it means</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td><span class="signal green" aria-label="Green">●</span></td>
              <td>≥ 100%</td>
              <td>At current pace, target is met or exceeded.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><span class="signal amber" aria-label="Amber">●</span></td>
              <td>60% – &lt;100%</td>
              <td>Target is missed but significant progress is made.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><span class="signal red" aria-label="Red">●</span></td>
              <td>&lt; 60%</td>
              <td>Target is missed badly. Recovery requires a step-change in build rate.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><span class="signal grey" aria-label="Grey">●</span></td>
              <td>n/a</td>
              <td>Insufficient or disputed data. See the card for details.</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h3 class="sub-heading">Why these thresholds</h3>

      <p>
        Green requires being on or ahead of pace — not close. These are
        large, long-lead infrastructure programmes. A 5% projected shortfall
        in mid-build is narrowly missing, not on track.
      </p>

      <p>
        Below 60%, catch-up requires more than doubling current build rate
        for the remaining years. That's not impossible, but it requires an
        external intervention — policy change, supply chain shift, or a
        significant injection of capital. Trend continuation won't get there.
        That's what red means.
      </p>

      <h3 class="sub-heading">What this method doesn't do</h3>

      <p>
        Linear extrapolation is a simplification. Build rates do accelerate
        as supply chains mature, so early-years lag against a linear
        projection isn't necessarily fatal. We use linear because it's the
        most transparent and defensible approach — anything more complex
        requires assumptions about future acceleration that are hard to
        justify without a forecasting model. We don't build forecasting
        models.
      </p>

      <p>
        Each scored card shows the underlying numbers alongside the traffic
        light. The signal is a starting point, not the whole story.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="meth-section" id="card-types">
      <h2 class="section-heading">Card types</h2>

      <p>Not everything on this dashboard can be scored against a 2030 target. There are three card types:</p>

      <div class="card-type-grid">
        <div class="card-type-item">
          <div class="card-type-badge scored">
            <span class="badge-dot"></span>
            Scored metric
          </div>
          <p>Infrastructure capacity measured against a 2030 target using gap-closure projection. Shows a traffic light, a delivery ratio, and a slip year. Battery storage, offshore wind, onshore wind, and solar will be scored metrics when added.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="card-type-item">
          <div class="card-type-badge context">
            <span class="badge-dot"></span>
            Context — no score
          </div>
          <p>A figure that provides useful context but has no government 2030 target to score against. Shows the figure, a historical sparkline, and an explainer. Grid constraint costs is a context card — there's no target for what this number should be, so assigning a traffic light would invent one.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="card-type-item">
          <div class="card-type-badge policy">
            <span class="badge-dot"></span>
            Policy status
          </div>
          <p>Policy proposals tracked by their regulatory status, not by a numerical target. Uses a different scoring rubric: implemented, consultation open, no active policy, or disputed. REMA / zonal pricing will be tracked this way.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p class="note">
        These three types use different evidence and different logic. They're
        presented separately and clearly labelled so you don't aggregate across
        them — an amber on battery storage (a measured shortfall against a
        numerical target) means something very different from an amber on
        zonal pricing (a qualitative status of an active consultation).
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="meth-section" id="constraint-costs">
      <h2 class="section-heading">Grid constraint costs</h2>

      <div class="metric-meta">
        <span class="meta-pill">Context card — no score</span>
        <span class="meta-pill">Source: NESO</span>
        <span class="meta-pill">Refreshes: monthly</span>
        <span class="meta-pill">Methodology: v0.2</span>
      </div>

      <h3 class="sub-heading">What this figure is</h3>

      <p>
        The card shows the total thermal constraint cost for the most recent
        complete UK financial year (April to March). As of May 2026, that is
        FY 2025–26: <strong>£1.96bn</strong>.
      </p>

      <p>
        Thermal constraint cost is the system cost of managing capacity limits
        on the transmission network. When there's more generation in one part
        of the country than the wires can carry, the system operator has to
        intervene: it pays some generators to reduce output, and simultaneously
        pays alternative generators elsewhere to increase output to compensate.
        The figure is the net cost of both sides of that transaction across a
        full year.
      </p>

      <p>
        It goes up when the grid is more congested. Congestion is directly tied
        to how fast transmission infrastructure is being built — which is why
        it's on this dashboard. For a plain English explanation of how
        constraint payments work, <a href="https://www.neso.energy/energy-101/electricity-explained/how-do-we-balance-grid/what-are-constraints-payments" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NESO's own explainer is the best starting point ↗</a>.
      </p>

      <h3 class="sub-heading">Why no traffic light</h3>

      <p>
        There's no government target for what this figure should be. Assigning
        a traffic light without a target would require us to invent one — which
        would be editorial, not measurement. The card shows the figure and its
        history so you can draw your own conclusions about the trend.
      </p>

      <h3 class="sub-heading">What's included in the figure</h3>

      <p>
        The NESO dataset contains four cost categories. This card publishes
        thermal constraint cost only. The other three categories are:
      </p>

      <ul class="meth-list">
        <li><strong>Voltage constraints cost</strong> — cost of managing voltage stability on the network. A different system problem from thermal limits.</li>
        <li><strong>Reducing largest loss cost</strong> — cost of actions taken to limit the impact of a sudden large generation loss. Zero across all current data.</li>
        <li><strong>Increasing system inertia cost</strong> — cost of maintaining frequency stability as more inverter-based generation comes online. Also zero across all current data.</li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        Thermal constraint cost is published because it's the category most
        directly tied to transmission bottlenecks and grid build-out. The other
        categories are fetched and stored but not displayed — they'll be added
        if the dashboard's scope expands to cover system balancing more broadly.
      </p>

      <h3 class="sub-heading">Time window</h3>

      <p>
        The headline figure is the sum over the most recent complete UK
        financial year. "Complete" means the year has fully closed — we don't
        publish a partial-year figure because it would be misleading to compare
        it against prior full years on the sparkline. Each April, the headline
        rolls forward to the just-closed year.
      </p>

      <h3 class="sub-heading">Data source and refresh</h3>

      <p>
        Source: <a href="https://www.neso.energy/data-portal/constraint-breakdown" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NESO Constraint Breakdown Costs and Volume ↗</a>.
        The dataset is published by the National Energy System Operator and
        updated monthly. We fetch it via NESO's open data API and validate the
        schema on every fetch — if the data shape changes unexpectedly, we
        preserve the last good data and flag the failure rather than
        silently publishing broken figures.
      </p>

      <h3 class="sub-heading">Known data caveat</h3>

      <div class="caveat-block">
        <span class="caveat-icon" aria-hidden="true">⚠</span>
        <p>
          The NESO source returned 364 rows for FY 2025–26, not the expected
          365. One day appears to be missing from the dataset. The headline
          figure is a sum of 364 days. We've logged this with NESO — it
          doesn't change the figure materially but it's disclosed here for
          transparency.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h3 class="sub-heading">The 2022–23 spike</h3>

      <p>
        The sparkline shows a significant spike in FY 2022–23 (£1.50bn
        thermal constraint cost). This attracted press coverage at the time.
        Figures cited in that coverage were typically higher than what appears
        here because they aggregated all four cost categories, not thermal
        only. The spike is real; the scale depends on which categories you
        include.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="meth-section" id="versioning">
      <h2 class="section-heading">Versioning and transparency</h2>

      <p>
        The methodology is versioned in the same git repository as the code
        and data. Every change to what gets shown, what gets scored, or how a
        metric is sourced gets a dated entry in the methodology changelog.
        The dashboard footer shows the active methodology version.
      </p>

      <p>
        Current version: <strong>v0.4</strong>. The full changelog is in
        the repository at <a href="https://github.com/nlostwanderer/dnls-netzero-dashboard/blob/main/methodology-changelog.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">methodology-changelog.md ↗</a>.
      </p>

      <p>
        If you spot an error, a disputed figure, or a source that's gone
        stale, the best route is a
        <a href="https://github.com/nlostwanderer/dnls-netzero-dashboard/issues" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GitHub issue ↗</a>.
        We'd rather be corrected publicly than wrong quietly.
      </p>
    </section>

  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .meth-page {
    padding-bottom: 4rem;
  }

  .page-intro {
    margin-bottom: 3rem;
    padding-bottom: 2.5rem;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);
  }

  .page-label {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 11px;
    letter-spacing: 0.12em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--accent-green);
    margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
  }

  .page-heading {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: 42px;
    font-weight: 500;
    font-style: italic;
    color: var(--text-primary);
    margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
    letter-spacing: -0.01em;
    line-height: 1.15;
  }

  .page-sub {
    font-family: var(--font-ui);
    font-size: 15px;
    color: var(--text-secondary);
    line-height: 1.7;
    max-width: 640px;
  }

  /* In-page nav */
  .meth-nav {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.5rem 1.5rem;
    margin-bottom: 3rem;
  }

  .meth-nav a {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 11px;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--text-muted);
    text-decoration: none;
  }

  .meth-nav a:hover { color: var(--accent-green); }

  /* Sections */
  .meth-section {
    margin-bottom: 4rem;
    padding-bottom: 4rem;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);
  }

  .meth-section:last-child {
    border-bottom: none;
  }

  .section-heading {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: 28px;
    font-weight: 500;
    font-style: italic;
    color: var(--text-primary);
    margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
    line-height: 1.2;
  }

  .sub-heading {
    font-family: var(--font-ui);
    font-size: 13px;
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: 0.06em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--text-secondary);
    margin: 2rem 0 0.75rem;
  }

  /* Prose */
  .meth-section p {
    font-family: var(--font-ui);
    font-size: 15px;
    color: var(--text-secondary);
    line-height: 1.75;
    max-width: 640px;
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
  }

  .meth-section p strong {
    color: var(--text-primary);
    font-weight: 700;
  }

  .meth-section p a,
  .note a {
    color: var(--accent-green);
    text-decoration: none;
  }

  .meth-section p a:hover,
  .note a:hover {
    text-decoration: underline;
  }

  .meth-section em {
    font-style: italic;
    color: var(--text-primary);
  }

  /* Lists */
  .meth-list {
    font-family: var(--font-ui);
    font-size: 15px;
    color: var(--text-secondary);
    line-height: 1.75;
    max-width: 620px;
    margin: 0 0 1rem 1.25rem;
  }

  .meth-list li {
    margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
  }

  .meth-list li strong {
    color: var(--text-primary);
  }

  /* Code */
  code {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 13px;
    background: var(--bg-surface);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 4px;
    padding: 2px 6px;
    color: var(--accent-green);
  }

  /* Traffic light table */
  .threshold-table-wrap {
    margin: 1.5rem 0;
    overflow-x: auto;
  }

  .threshold-table {
    width: 100%;
    max-width: 640px;
    border-collapse: collapse;
    font-family: var(--font-ui);
    font-size: 14px;
  }

  .threshold-table th {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 10px;
    letter-spacing: 0.1em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--text-muted);
    text-align: left;
    padding: 0.5rem 1rem 0.5rem 0;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);
  }

  .threshold-table td {
    padding: 0.75rem 1rem 0.75rem 0;
    color: var(--text-secondary);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);
    vertical-align: top;
    line-height: 1.5;
  }

  .threshold-table tr:last-child td { border-bottom: none; }

  .signal {
    font-size: 18px;
    line-height: 1;
  }

  .signal.green { color: #5DCAA5; }
  .signal.amber { color: #EF9F27; }
  .signal.red { color: #e05252; }
  .signal.grey { color: #5b5b62; }

  /* Card type grid */
  .card-type-grid {
    display: grid;
    gap: 1rem;
    margin: 1.5rem 0;
    max-width: 640px;
  }

  .card-type-item {
    background: var(--bg-surface);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 10px;
    padding: 1.25rem;
  }

  .card-type-item p {
    font-size: 14px;
    margin-bottom: 0;
    margin-top: 0.75rem;
  }

  .card-type-badge {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 5px;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 10px;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    border-radius: 4px;
    padding: 3px 8px;
  }

  .card-type-badge .badge-dot {
    width: 5px;
    height: 5px;
    border-radius: 50%;
    flex-shrink: 0;
  }

  .card-type-badge.scored {
    color: #5DCAA5;
    background: rgba(93, 202, 165, 0.1);
    border: 1px solid rgba(93, 202, 165, 0.25);
  }

  .card-type-badge.scored .badge-dot { background: #5DCAA5; }

  .card-type-badge.context {
    color: #EF9F27;
    background: rgba(239, 159, 39, 0.1);
    border: 1px solid rgba(239, 159, 39, 0.25);
  }

  .card-type-badge.context .badge-dot { background: #EF9F27; }

  .card-type-badge.policy {
    color: #9b9b9b;
    background: rgba(155, 155, 155, 0.1);
    border: 1px solid rgba(155, 155, 155, 0.2);
  }

  .card-type-badge.policy .badge-dot { background: #9b9b9b; }

  .note {
    font-family: var(--font-ui);
    font-size: 13px;
    color: var(--text-muted);
    line-height: 1.7;
    max-width: 640px;
    border-left: 2px solid var(--border);
    padding-left: 1rem;
    margin-top: 1rem;
  }

  /* Metric meta pills */
  .metric-meta {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    margin-bottom: 1.75rem;
  }

  .meta-pill {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 10px;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--text-muted);
    background: var(--bg-surface);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 4px;
    padding: 3px 8px;
  }

  /* Caveat block */
  .caveat-block {
    display: flex;
    align-items: flex-start;
    gap: 10px;
    background: rgba(186, 117, 23, 0.07);
    border: 1px solid rgba(186, 117, 23, 0.2);
    border-radius: 8px;
    padding: 1rem 1.25rem;
    margin: 1rem 0;
    max-width: 640px;
  }

  .caveat-icon {
    font-size: 14px;
    color: #ba7517;
    flex-shrink: 0;
    margin-top: 2px;
  }

  .caveat-block p {
    font-size: 13px;
    color: #7a6a45;
    margin: 0;
    line-height: 1.6;
  }
</style>
